## 8. HUMANOID ROBOT THESIS

### 8.1 The Big Picture

Camillo identifies **Tesla (Optimus), Figure AI, and 1X** as the leaders in the humanoid robot market. He is invested in the sector but warns Wall Street timelines are "completely wrong."

### 8.2 Timeline Assessment

| Milestone | Estimated Date |
|-----------|---------------|
| **Commercially viable prototype** | Late 2027 or 2028 |
| **Actual mass deployment begins** | ~2030 |
| **Supply/demand imbalance** | 10-15 years stretching into 2030s |

### 8.3 The Bottleneck

**It is NOT manufacturing. It is deployment.**

Integrating generalized robots into complex workflows of legacy companies (FedEx, Walmart) requires:
- Years of strategy
- Digitizing inventory systems
- Preparing workforces
- Safety protocols and liability frameworks

### 8.4 The KPI Thresholds

| KPI | Target | Current Status |
|-----|--------|---------------|
| **Task completion rate** | 99.5%+ | **No company has met this** |
| **Human-equivalent output speed** | 80% of a human | Not yet achieved |
| **Hours between faults (uptime)** | Reliable for production | Not production-ready |
| **Durability** | 3-5 years | Untested |
| **Human-to-bot ratio** | Minimal supervision | Currently requires near-constant human oversight |

### 8.5 Current State by Company

| Company | Position | Assessment |
|---------|----------|------------|
| **Tesla Optimus** | Building from ground up | V2 had "numerous critical flaws on both hardware and AI" — rebuilding for V3/V4. Significantly behind Elon's timelines. |
| **Figure AI** | Impressive demos | Building ~1 robot/hour for training/showmanship. Not scalable — still a full generation off. |
| **1X** | Intriguing design | Tendon-driven, soft-body design (65 lbs, safe for home). Heavily reliant on **human teleoperation** for training data. |

